<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.1.4: Exploring a System of Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00365</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.1.4: Exploring a System of Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6f9f1765-cc24-4e3a-89f9-cca3e758f95e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>At a fabric store, fabrics are sold by the yard. A dressmaker spent $36.35 on 4.25 yards of silk and cotton fabrics for a dress. Silk is $16.90 per yard, and cotton is $4 per yard.</para>
<para>Here is a system of equations that represents the constraints in the situation.</para>
<para>\(\displaystyle \begin{cases} \begin {align} x + \hspace{2.2mm} y &amp;=
  \hspace{2mm} 4.25\\ 16.90x + 4y &amp;= 36.35 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    What does the solution to the system represent?
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. The solution represents the combination of lengths of silk and cotton (in yards) that meet both constraints: they add up to 4.25 yards, and the cost of buying both is $36.35.</para>

<list start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Find the solution to the system of equations. 
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>1.5 yards of silk and 2.75 yards of cotton.</para>

<list start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Explain or show how you know.
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
<list>
  <item>
    The graphs of the equations intersect at 

 ((1.5,2.75)\)
    <image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/00b4ac90c051ada23ff5235d9161f8ff72d6c048" alt="Graph of a linear system. cotton (yards), silk (yards)."/></item>
  <item>  Solving by using a table gives \(x=1.5\) and \(y=2.75\).</item>
</list>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>The cool-down in lesson 2.4 gives another opportunity for students to discuss the meaning of a solution in context, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para>
</section>
</content>
